# O'Reilly-style markdown (strict)

All concept notes, guides, and site markdown follow an **O'Reilly Animal Guide** voice and format. Visual rendering uses `article.prose` + `src/styles/prose.css` (sans-serif body).

## Typography & HTML/CSS contract

- Body and headings are **sans-serif** (site `--font-sans`). Never introduce serif stacks for article prose.
- Code and math stay monospace / KaTeX; do not use monospace for ordinary body text.
- Articles render inside `<article class="prose">`. Do not invent alternate article typography.

## Headings

- Sentence case only: `## Latency metrics`, not `## Latency Metrics`.
- Prefer numbered chapter-style sections when the post is a guided note: `## 1. …`, `### Prefill vs decode`.
- Em dash for subtitle: `## 1. The mental model — three resources`.
- No emoji in headings. No trailing `#` decorations.

## Emphasis

- Prefer *italics* for terms on first use and light emphasis.
- Use **bold** sparingly (key term once, or a single takeaway) — not whole sentences, not every table cell.
- Do not bold entire list lead-ins by default; italics or plain text are fine.

## Figures

```html
<figure>

![Alt text describing the figure](./images/name.png)

<figcaption><span class="figure-label">Figure N.</span> Caption in sentence case with no trailing period</figcaption>
</figure>
```

- Caption: sentence case, **no trailing period**.
- Label format exactly: `Figure N.` (bold via `.figure-label`).
- Alt text is descriptive, not the caption duplicated.

## Tables

- Clear header row; left-align text; right-align pure numeric columns when helpful.
- Keep cells short; put explanation in surrounding prose, not megacells.
- Avoid stuffing `**bold**` into every cell.

## Lists & structure

- Short paragraphs (2–4 sentences). Lead with the point.
- Use lists for parallel items; use tables for comparisons; use prose for narrative.
- Horizontal rules (`---`) sparingly — prefer heading hierarchy.
- Math in `$$` / `$` KaTeX; define symbols in a small table when there are more than two.

## Voice

- Tutorial / practitioner tone: concrete numbers, worked examples, “why it matters.”
- Avoid slide-deck fragments, telegram bullets, and unexplained jargon.
- Link related posts in-flow: `[GPU Architecture](./gpu-architecture)`.

## ❌ Avoid

```markdown
## Latency Metrics — TTFT/ITL/TPS!!!
**TTFT** is **really** important. **ITL** is **also** important.
---
- **Runtime:** stuff
- **Infrastructure:** stuff
```

## ✅ Prefer

```markdown
## 2. Latency metrics — TTFT, ITL, and TPS

When a user sends a prompt, two clocks matter: how long until the first token, and how fast the rest streams.

- *TTFT* (time to first token) — delay until the first output token
- *ITL* (inter-token latency) — time between consecutive tokens after the first
```
